You can do this with a loop that keeps an absolute schedule instead of “sleeping after each run,” so the execution time doesn’t accumulate as lag.

A Perl solution from the thread is:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;
use Time::HiRes qw/time sleep/;

sub launch {
    return if fork;
    exec @_;
    die "Couldn't exec";
}

$SIG{CHLD} = 'IGNORE';

my $interval = shift;
my $start = time();
while (1) {
    launch(@ARGV);
    $start += $interval;
    sleep $start - time();
}
```

Run it like this:

```sh
perl timer.pl 1 date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'
```

It schedules the next run based on the original start time, so it doesn’t keep drifting over time the way a simple `sleep 1` loop can.

It’s noted, though, that this still can’t guarantee exact second boundaries; there’s always some overhead, and under heavy load it may fall behind if `fork()` or the command itself takes too long.

You can pipe the colored output through a Perl filter to strip ANSI escape codes:

`command-that-produces-colored-output | perl -pe 's/\e\[?.*?[\@-~]//g' > outfile`

If you want a reusable script, this works:

```perl
#! /usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;

while (<>) {
  s/\e\[?.*?[\@-~]//g;
  print;
}
```

If you only want to remove color codes and keep other ANSI sequences, use:

`perl -pe 's/\e\[[\d;]*m//g'`

A simple way is to strip ANSI/VT100 escape sequences rather than trying to match specific color codes. For example, this Perl filter removes common terminal control sequences from `script` output:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
while (<>) {
    s/ \e[ #%()*+\-.\/]. |
       \e\[ [ -?]* [@-~] |
       \e\] .*? (?:\e\\|[\a\x9c]) |
       \e[P^_] .*? (?:\e\\|\x9c) |
       \e. //xg;
    print;
}
```

If you also need to handle some non-ASCII control characters, there’s a broader version:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
while (<>) {
    s/ \e[ #%()*+\-.\/]. |
       (?:\e\[|\x9b) [ -?]* [@-~] |
       (?:\e\]|\x9d) .*? (?:\e\\|[\a\x9c]) |
       (?:\e[P^_]|[\x90\x9e\x9f]) .*? (?:\e\\|\x9c) |
       \e.|[\x80-\x9f] //xg;
    print;
}
```

This is meant to remove terminal escape sequences such as color changes from recorded output.

Use a finally block if you want the secret file deleted no matter where the script fails.

For example:

try {
    set foo [lindex $argv 0]
    set bar [lindex $argv 1]
    set secret [lindex $argv 2]

    if { ! ([file exists "$foo"] && [file executable "$foo"]) } {
        puts stderr "$foo does not exist or is not executable"
        exit 2
    }

    if { ! ([file exists "$bar"] && [file executable "$bar"]) } {
        puts stderr "$bar does not exist or is not executable"
        exit 3
    }

    if { ! ([file exists "$secret"] && [file readable "$secret"]) } {
        puts stderr "$secret does not exist or is not readable"
        exit 4
    }

    # Read secret, do foo, do bar, etc.
} finally {
    file delete $secret
}

If you also want to catch errors explicitly, you can add an on error handler, but it’s not required for the cleanup.

