Yes. The Perl approach is to keep the relevant part from the current record and apply it to the next matching records.

A solution along these lines is:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

my @repeat;

while (my $line = <>) {
    chomp $line;

    if (my ($first, $second) = $line =~ /^MTD.{8}(...)(.*?) /) {
        $repeat[0] //= $first;
        $repeat[1]  = $second;
        substr $line, 11, 3, $repeat[0];
    }

    if ($line =~ /^([AB])\1 /) {
        substr $line, 11, 0, $repeat[0] . $repeat[1];
    }

    print "$line\n";
}
```

What it does:
- Reads the file line by line.
- When it finds an `MTD` record, it captures the needed part and stores it.
- For following `AA` or `BB` records, it inserts that stored value into the line.
- Writes the transformed lines to output.

If your fixed-length positions differ, adjust the `substr` offsets and the regex accordingly.

