If your system has GNU find, FreeBSD find, or macOS/OSX find, one option is:

find /path/to/directory -newerBt '2009-01-01' ! -newerBt '2010-01-01' -type f -exec mv {} /path/to/target/ \;

If you want to use modification time instead of birth time, replace `B` with `m`:

find /path/to/directory -newermt '2009-01-01' ! -newermt '2010-01-01' -type f -exec mv {} /path/to/target/ \;

If your `find` doesn’t support date comparisons directly, use timestamp files:

touch -t 200901010000 /tmp/from
touch -t 201001010000 /tmp/to
find /path/to/directory -newer /tmp/from ! -newer /tmp/to -type f -exec mv {} /path/to/target/ \;

Run it first without the `mv` to make sure the matched files are the ones you want. If you only want files in the top directory and not subdirectories, add `-mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1` after the directory name.

