You can use a rename loop that changes each filename based on a pattern, for example with Perl:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl

opendir(DIR, ".") or die "$@:$!";
while ($in = readdir(DIR)) {
  next unless -f $in;
  ($out = $in) =~ s/[^a-zA-Z0-9._-]//g;
  warn "$@:$!" unless rename $in, $out;
}
closedir(DIR);
```

This removes characters outside `a-zA-Z0-9._-` from each filename.

If you want to keep regular UTF-8 characters like umlauts and spaces, this exact filter would be too strict; the closest supported guidance here is to adjust the character class so it only removes the invalid characters you want gone.

