If you want timestamps on each line, the supported guidance here is to centralize output through logging functions instead of redirecting all `STDOUT`/`STDERR` directly and trying to prefix every `print` manually.

For example:

```perl
sub logmsg {
  print scalar localtime() . " @_\n";
}

sub logerr {
  print STDERR scalar localtime() . "ERROR: @_\n";
}
```

Then use `logmsg()` for normal output and `logerr()` for errors. That way, every message gets a timestamp automatically.

If you are already capturing `STDOUT` and `STDERR` like this:

```perl
open STDOUT, '>>', $log_file or die "Can't redirect STDOUT: $!";
open STDERR, ">&STDOUT" or die "Can't add to STDOUT: $!";
main();
```

that will send both streams into the log file, but it won’t add timestamps by itself. The closest supported approach is to stop relying only on redirected `STDOUT`/`STDERR` and instead route your messages through functions like `logmsg` and `logerr`, where you can prepend `scalar localtime()`.

It also notes that if you later want, those functions could write directly to the file or to something like syslog instead.

You can’t selectively timestamp only some `STDOUT`/`STDERR` lines just by doing:

```perl
open STDOUT, '>>', "$log_file";
open STDERR, '>&STDOUT';
```

That redirects everything, but it doesn’t insert per-line timestamps.

The supported approach is to stop printing directly everywhere and instead route output through your own logging functions, for example:

```perl
sub logmsg {
  print scalar localtime() . " @_\n";
}

sub logerr {
  print STDERR scalar localtime() . "ERROR: @_\n";
}

sub debug {
  if ($debug) {
    print "DEBUG: @_\n";
  }
}
```

Then use `logmsg` for normal output, `logerr` for errors, and `debug` for debug messages. That lets you add timestamps only where you want them, and also makes it easy to change later where those messages go.

If your goal is specifically to capture all output from `main()` and timestamp every line automatically, the evidence does not provide a direct `script`-command solution for that. The closest guidance it supports is to handle timestamps in your own output functions rather than relying on redirection alone.

If you just want to turn each row into separate lines, awk can do that directly:

```sh
awk -F, '{ for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) print $i; print "" }' file
```

That prints each comma-separated field on its own line, with a blank line between rows.

If you meant “make rows into columns” in the sense of transposing the file, awk/sed alone is not a good fit for CSV in general, especially if fields can contain quotes or commas inside quoted text. The related solution here shows that CSV gets tricky fast, and it uses preprocessing around awk to handle quoted fields safely.

For a simple CSV with no embedded commas in quoted fields, you can transpose in awk like this:

```sh
awk -F, '{
  for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) a[NR, i] = $i
  if (NF > max) max = NF
}
END {
  for (i = 1; i <= max; i++) {
    for (r = 1; r <= NR; r++) {
      printf "%s%s", a[r, i], (r < NR ? OFS : ORS)
    }
  }
}' file
```

But if your data is real CSV with quotes and embedded commas, the safer approach is to use a CSV-aware tool rather than sed/awk.

