}
```

That way the script runs only when you intentionally enter that project.

On Linux, the closest supported approach is to watch the file or directory with `inotify` and run your script when a relevant event occurs. For a “file changes” or upload-style trigger, watch the target directory and react to events like `CREATE` or `CLOSE_WRITE`.

A simple pattern is:

```sh
#!/bin/sh

EVENT=$(inotifywait --format '%e' /path/to/file-or-dir)
[ $? != 0 ] && exit

case "$EVENT" in
  MODIFY) echo "file modified!" ;;
  DELETE_SELF) echo "file deleted!" ;;
  CREATE) echo "file created!" ;;
esac
```

If you want to run a command each time a file is uploaded into a directory, use `inotifywait` from `inotify-tools` on that directory and trigger your bash script when the desired event happens.

